 every  year but only 5% of it is effectively  recycled much of it ending up being  junked in our oceans  Prince Charles is a passionate  conservationist his love affair with the  sea started as a young boy and it grew  while it was a sailor in the Royal Navy  all these problems are accelerating and  multiplying at the same time and what we  try to please you don't wait until it's  a total disaster before you do something  it is a terrible aspect of human nature  that we we wait for proof so what I was  trying to say was we're testing the  world to destruction so another time you  have actually tested it and discovered  oh yes there's the evidence he's too  late  but but the oceans matter huge it but  the the problem is it's so I decided out  of mind what we do on the land we don't  realize go straight out to sea so all  the chemicals and pesticides and  everything else some nitrates we pour on  the land orders to be able to see very  often many parts that will form dead  zones and everything else one of the  princes key concerns is sustainable  fishing populations have been plundered  by unregulated overfishing and these  stocks need time to grow again but  there's also a real worry about ghost  nets discarded lines in gear continuing  to cause massive damage long after their  initial use has been served it's a  serious problem even here for the small  island that is hosting this year's ocean  conference this is some of the plastic  fishing gear that's been abandoned by a  trawler off the coast of malta after  snagging on a reef yet it continues to  catch marine life volunteer divers use  inflated bags to lift the nets to the  surface 500 kilos from one area in just  two days but it's nowhere near enough  worldwide 640 thousand tonnes of fishing  equipment is lost at sea every year this  has been done there probably for a few  years already because  there is some growth on it but you can  see that it's easy for animals to get  entangled in it often we find new nets  which has been done there for one or two  weeks already in those nets you plant so  much marine life getting a tangle and  attracting other animals what you need  to do is organize the fishing in a way  that enables the fish and everything  that the fish depend on to survive in  their ecosystems is called an ecosystem  based approach to fisheries management  is no good just letting people fish  everywhere with their bigger and bigger  boats with you know cleverer and  cleverer technology to find and suck up  every single last fish that's crazy  that's not conservation it's madness  well that's what's been going on and  I'll say you just stop fishing you have  to do it in a way that will actually  produce more fish for us all but by  managing it properly  Prince Charles talks about a blue  economy a business model that takes its  commitment to the aquatic environment  seriously and driven by this  understanding sky has continued with its  promise to put the environment at the  heart of what it does  plastic is flowing into our oceans at an  unprecedented rate we all know time is  running out we all have a duty to act  and a responsibility to take action now  skies ocean rescue campaign was launched  in January this year it's already  engaged with over six million people  plastic Asst a 10 meter whale made from  the same amount of plastic and enters  the ocean every second has toured the UK  helping to spread awareness the campaign  has seen many communities join forces  volunteers inspired and motivated to  join hands and organize local cleanups  Sky's chief executive officer Jeremy  Derrick has also announced a number of  key pledges that will not only see sky  continue to take the lead on  environmentally sound business practices  but help its business partners in supply  chain transform their operations first  of all that is a company we are going to  be free of single-use plastics by 2020  so right throughout our business whether  it's in our operations our sup